{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310558,"nonce":"s8qgYpkxRhTMGzUC3d7c47DKaAY=","title":"Why no class ever had an owned field, and the three stacked defects in the owned-field analysis behind binarytrees' 4+ GB","message":"**IN PROGRESS, uncommitted.** An object stored into another object's field is\nnever freed. `scripts/check_composition_leak.sh` is the oracle and exits 1:\ncontrol flat at ~30 MB across 2M/4M iterations, wrapped grows 94 → 158 MB, so\nroughly **32 bytes per iteration** never returned. Same shape as binarytrees'\n4+ GB (`new TreeNode(bottomUpTree(..), bottomUpTree(..), item)`).\n\nThe deep-release machinery already exists (`haxe_object_free_deep` +\n`OWNED_FIELD_MASKS`). It reclaims nothing because `compute_owned_fields`\nproduces an **entirely empty mask set** — not merely incomplete. Three\nindependent defects, stacked; the first two are fixed and the count of\ndisqualifying uses dropped 3 → 1.\n\n1. **FIXED — the constructor poisons its own field.** Evidence is combined with\n   `*e \u0026= ok`, so ONE false disqualifies a field permanently. Inside a\n   constructor the store is `this.c = c`, writing a **parameter**, which is by\n   definition not a fresh allocation, so `fresh_single_holder` returns false.\n   Every class initialises through a constructor, so every class cast that vote\n   — hence *no* class in *any* program had an owned field. Fixed by skipping the\n   callee half: whether it is a handover is decided at the CALL SITE, by the\n   `CallDirect` arm that exists for it.\n2. **FIXED — initialisation counted as escape.** A fresh object always has its\n   type-id header written and its constructor called before being handed over,\n   and both counted as other holders. Now distinguished by direction:\n   `Store { ptr: derived }` writes INTO the object (initialisation, skip);\n   `Store { value: derived }` writes it into something else (a real second\n   holder, still counts). The constructor call is skipped only when the object\n   is `args[0]` and `param_retention` says that parameter is not retained.\n3. **FIXED — depended on an analysis that does not run.** The fix for (2)\n   consulted `param_retention`, which is **opt-in behind `RZT_PARAM_RETENTION`\n   and empty by default** (\"while the remaining soundness hole is bisected\").\n   Every lookup returned `None`, which the code read as \"escapes\". The\n   diagnostic printed `param0 kept=None` — absence, not `false`. Replaced with\n   `receiver_escapes`, which asks the callee's body directly via\n   `param_retained` with an empty nested map (so anything reached through a\n   nested call stays conservative).\n4. **FIXED — a dead load made every constructor look leaky.** `this.x = v`\n   lowers to the store **plus a load of the field straight back**, whose result\n   is never used:\n\n   ```\n   fn @Cell.new($0: *void, $1: i32) -\u003e void {\n       $3 = gep i32 $0, [1] ; store $3, $1\n       $5 = gep i32 $0, [1] ; $6 = load i32 $5   \u003c-- $6 dead\n   }\n   ```\n\n   `param_retained` treats a load through a parameter as an escape (an interior\n   pointer it cannot track), and **`InsertFreePass` is added FIRST in\n   `PassManager::for_level`, before any `DeadCodeEliminationPass`**, at every\n   level — so it always sees the un-DCE'd body. Fixed by only counting a load\n   as an escape when something actually reads the loaded value.\n\n   **The dead load is NOT a runtime cost — verified, and an earlier claim to\n   the contrary is wrong.** `Load` is absent from `has_side_effects`, so MIR\n   DCE deletes it, and the optimised LLVM IR for a field-writing method has no\n   such load (the method is inlined away outright). Commit 862ba8e6's message\n   says \"every field assignment in the language pays a redundant address\n   computation and load\" — that is FALSE. Its only bite is on analyses that run\n   BEFORE DCE, and `InsertFreePass` is deliberately added FIRST in\n   `PassManager::for_level`, so it is the one that sees it. Do not spend effort\n   removing the load in lowering for performance; there is none to gain.\n\n**STILL OPEN.** After all four, the fresh `Cell` is accepted (`IrId(20)` no\nlonger rejected) but `Box`'s field is still REJECTED, now by `IrId(21)` /\n`IrId(3)` voting \"not a fresh allocation\" from a site not yet localised. Next\nprobe: print the enclosing function and instruction for each `note()` that\nvotes false, not just the value id — the current diagnostic names the value but\nnot where the vote came from.\n\n**Judgement:** four real defects, each invisible until the one above it was\nfixed, and the leak still reproduces. This is no longer a targeted fix; treat\nthe next attempt as a review of the whole owned-field derivation rather than\nanother patch. All of it is UNCOMMITTED and unverified against the test suite.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): Composition leak: owned fields: the mask set is EMPTY because every constructor poisons its own field (evidence is ANDed); 2 of 3 defects fixed, uses 3→1. `RZT_DBG_OWNED=1` now prints per-stage reasons.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_composition_leak_owned_fields` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}